-- title:
--   Cramér–Lundberg surplus increments are independent of the natural past and stationary
-- statement:
--   Let $(\tau_i)_{i\ge0}$ be i.i.d. exponential interarrival times of rate $\lambda>0$ and $(Y_i)_{i\ge0}$ i.i.d. claims with law $F$, all jointly independent (a compound Poisson driver). Let $S_t=\sum_{i<N_t}Y_i$ be the aggregate claims, where $N_t$ counts the arrivals by time $t$. For a premium rate $c\in\mathbb R$, consider the surplus process started at $0$,
--   $$X_t = c\,t - S_t,\qquad t\ge 0,$$
--   and its natural filtration $\mathcal F^X_s=\sigma(X_u : u\le s)$. Then for all $0\le s\le t$:
--
--   1. the increment $X_t - X_s$ is independent of $\mathcal F^X_s$;
--   2. the increment is stationary: $X_t - X_s$ has the same law as $X_{t-s}$.
--
--   These are the independent- and stationary-increment properties of the Lévy process $X$, stated for the natural filtration of the concrete compound-Poisson-with-drift path. They are the input for martingale arguments in the Cramér–Lundberg and M/G/1 models, such as the exponential martingale $e^{\theta X_t - \psi(\theta)t}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $X$ is `riskSurplus arrival claims premium 0`, the natural filtration is `naturalSigma X s`, independence is `Indep` between the σ-algebra generated by the increment and `naturalSigma X s`, and stationarity is equality of push-forward laws. The binder `arrival` is the interarrival sequence of the driver.
-- source:
--   Kyprianou (2006), Introductory Lectures on Fluctuations of Lévy Processes with Applications, §1.2.2 (compound Poisson processes are Lévy processes) and §1.3.1–1.3.2 (Cramér–Lundberg / M/G/1 surplus $X_t = ct - S_t$ as a spectrally negative Lévy process); used in Exercise 1.9(ii), p. 31.

